WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.570
A shopkeeper in Uttar Pradesh loses power almost every evening.

2
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.518
When she complains, her memory is all she has.

3
00:00:07.938 --> 00:00:11.218
But independent monitors, run by Prayas (Energy Group),

4
00:00:11.238 --> 00:00:13.218
recorded the voltage every minute.

5
00:00:13.238 --> 00:00:16.601
About 103 million real readings.

6
00:00:17.021 --> 00:00:22.541
That is where VoltProof comes in. It analyses them entirely inside Exasol Personal.

7
00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:25.161
The files are loaded exactly as published,

8
00:00:25.181 --> 00:00:27.641
every minute is classified in SQL,

9
00:00:27.661 --> 00:00:30.747
and every power cut is found by one window function.

10
00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:35.247
Finding every cut used to take 665 s.

11
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.427
Now it takes 35.6 s.

12
00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:41.498
And every statement is matched against Exasol's own audit log.

13
00:00:41.918 --> 00:00:48.758
Here is what it found: at least 366.2 hours without supply in one month, at one monitor.

14
00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:52.818
And 17 monitors losing supply within the same two minutes.

15
00:00:52.838 --> 00:00:56.474
Coincidence is not cause, but it is a lead worth checking.

16
00:00:57.194 --> 00:00:59.810
Now, let's see how VoltProof works.

17
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.300
First, we have to upload the CSV file here, but for the demo,

18
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.600
we have already uploaded a Harvard-given CSV file from Prayas (Energy Group).

19
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.050
So, let's start.

20
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.940
This is a place in Uttar Pradesh, and this month alone, this particular month,

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.340
there has been an outage of 366.2 hours.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.080
And if you scroll down, we see how much outage time there was this month,

23
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.960
and on the exact dates too.

24
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.970
So now, let's take the 25th as an example.

25
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.390
We can see that at this time there was power, and here, there was no power.

26
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.680
If we scroll down, we can see exactly at which time there was no power.

27
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.230
There were power outages at exactly… let's just take this time.

28
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.590
And this is the evidence that shows exactly what happened.

29
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.180
Here, it is seen that within two minutes,

30
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.170
there were other power outages in other places, not just here.

31
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.900
Now, how was this computed?

32
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.740
This was computed through Exasol.

33
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.250
What it does is that Exasol takes the statements from SQL and calculates the time on its own.

34
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.370
Now, we get back.

35
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.500
We can see that we can generate the evidence report here,

36
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.960
and the evidence report is in three types: PDF, HTML and JSON.

37
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.810
So, let's check PDF.

38
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.160
This is a report,

39
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:27.240
and this can be used by both the utility company and the shopkeeper

40
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:33.680
to get the exact evidence and to show where the issues were.

41
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:40.940
At the same time, the utility company can take this as a reference

42
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.350
to make better adjustments.

43
00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:49.020
Now, this is the methodology that we use.

44
00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:50.690
Thank you.
